WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.460
VIVIAN ZHONG: Hi, my
name is Vivian Zhong.

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:06.450
I'm a junior working in the Alm
Lab doing microbiome research.

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.560
And my current project revolves
around this treatment called

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.910
fecal microbiota transplant.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:15.660
This treatment involves taking
the stool from a healthy donor

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.870
and transplanting into
the gut of a sick patient.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.370
And this is a really
effective cure

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:24.460
for a disease called
recurrent c-diff infection.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.340
However, it's not
really that effective

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.890
in many other diseases
that are similarly related

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.870
to the microbiome.

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:36.630
And we want to look into what
affects the efficacy of FMT.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.970
What factors play
into whether or not

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:43.560
the bacteria in the donor's
stool colonize the patient gut?

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:46.950
And so the two factors that
we looked into in my project--

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:50.790
one is whether or not the
stool, prior to transplantation,

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.040
was exposed oxygen or not.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.440
The idea behind this
is that your gut is

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.660
a very oxygen-deficient
environment,

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.110
but the usual processing
method currently used

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:04.080
in clinical trials
and in medical centers

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.590
involves exposing the stool
to a large amount of oxygen.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:10.370
And so perhaps that's
enough to alter

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:13.740
the microbiome composition
of the donor stool sample

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:17.520
enough that it impacts how
well the bacteria colonize

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.236
the patient.

00:01:19.236 --> 00:01:21.360
And the second factor is
to look at whether or not,

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.350
perhaps, the sheer
difference in the donor.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.640
Since everybody has a
very different microbiota

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.670
composition, perhaps just
inter-donor variations

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:35.370
have a large effect on how
the donor's stool interacts

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:38.080
with the patient.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:41.800
So did a clinical trial with
three donors and 11 patients.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:45.150
We took stool samples from the
donor and from the patient,

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.860
both before and at
four time points

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.560
after the treatment was given.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.790
And long story short, we
looked at the composition

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.470
of the stool samples.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:59.040
And so far, it doesn't seem like
there's a really large signal

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.160
to indicate any difference
between patients that were

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.850
treated with oxygen-exposed or
not-exposed-to-oxygen stool.

00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:11.370
And what might be either
covering any signal

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:17.130
from that effect, or which
might be more important than

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.710
whether or not the stool
was exposed to oxygen,

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:21.960
is the inter-donor variations.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.910
Preliminary results
indicate that the stool

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.790
from one of the donors
was perhaps worse

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:31.140
than the other two
donors for the bacteria

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:33.750
to colonize in the patient gut.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.300
And in the future I'm
going to do some more

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.045
analysis of the
data, looking more

00:02:39.045 --> 00:02:41.910
at specific bacteria,
whether those perhaps

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:45.240
do differ between oxygen
and non-oxygen-exposed,

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:49.440
and also, looking more at are
the donor-specific effects

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.670
because of the differences
in the composition?

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:56.240
Are they perhaps artifacts
of how they were processed?

00:02:56.240 --> 00:03:00.320
To try to get to the bottom
of the mechanism behind FMT.

